The v2 planner contains `schemaVersion: 2`, a nullable term, courses, weekly lectures, and a completion map. Lecture `title` is optional legacy compatibility metadata: existing titles are retained in older records/backups but ignored by the UI, while new sessions omit it. No database reset or schema-version bump is needed. Tag edits preserve session IDs/completion keys. Weekdays are Monday=0 through Sunday=6. Occurrences are generated rather than stored as database rows; equal start times sort by course name, tags and ID without requiring a title.

SQLite stores one planner JSON record with a revision and update timestamp. Saves use compare-and-update revisions; stale tabs receive HTTP 409 instead of silently overwriting newer data. Existing v1 planners/backups upgrade while preserving lecture IDs and completion keys. Backup format is `{ format: 'lecture-ledger', version: 2, data: planner }`; restore accepts versions 1 and 2.

Endpoints: `GET /api/planner` returns data/revision, `PUT /api/planner` saves validated data/revision, and `GET /api/backup` downloads JSON. Managed production startup also enables `GET /api/control/status` and `POST /api/control/shutdown`; both require the current instance's random bearer token, which is stored only in private ignored runtime state. Cross-site control requests are rejected. Shutdown uses the authenticated instance rather than sending signals to a saved PID. The server binds only to `127.0.0.1`, checks allowed Host and write Origin, limits request bodies to 2 MB, and serves only the built frontend in production. Unknown API routes and attempts to access private files are rejected. There is one local planner, shared by browsers using the same server; this is not a multi-user account service.

## Running, storage, and backups

On this Linux computer, desktop and applications-menu shortcuts are installed at `/home/dz/Desktop/Lecture Tracker.desktop` and `/home/dz/.local/share/applications/lecture-tracker.desktop`. They launch `desktop.sh`, opening a dedicated Electron window. Close the window, click sidebar Quit app or choose File → Quit Lecture Tracker to shut down. Reopening focuses the same instance. Ctrl+1/2/3 navigates Overview/Courses/Catch up, Ctrl+, opens settings and Ctrl+Q quits. Some desktops require Allow Launching once. **Do not direct this user to desktop-file right-click controls: their screenshot confirmed that menu does not show application actions.** The app window's own controls are the supported path. Reinstall shortcuts with `./app.sh install-shortcut` after moving/cloning the repository. Installation replaces old Lecture Ledger shortcut files only when their ownership marker and executable match this checkout; other shortcuts are retained. Generated files contain the checkout path and stay outside Git.

Taskbar launches use `lecture-tracker.desktop`; the native desktop name and StartupWMClass match `lecture-tracker`. The launcher runs Electron against the project manifest and waits for IPC startup acknowledgement before detaching. Early fatal errors are reported with the current launch's log. Ubuntu restricts unprivileged user namespaces in the normal desktop context, while Codex's AppArmor context permits them: testing only inside Codex can conceal real taskbar failures. `./setup-sandbox.sh` uses pkexec administrator authentication to install a per-checkout, exact-executable AppArmor userns permission under `/etc/apparmor.d/lecture-tracker-<path-hash>`. It retains Chromium sandboxing, avoids a setuid binary/global sysctl change, rejects policy metacharacters, validates before loading, and refuses to replace a different existing profile. Moving the checkout requires setup again. On 14 October, the user completed authentication and `/etc/apparmor.d/lecture-tracker-29f60fbd7cc1c2b4` was installed/loaded, owned by root with mode 644. Normal desktop-service launching passed using the installed profile, with renderer Seccomp mode 2; the global namespace restriction remains 1 and chrome-sandbox remains user-owned mode 755. The existing pinned desktop ID is correct; re-pinning is unnecessary.

From the active repository:

```sh
./open.sh
./stop.sh
```

`open.sh`, `start.sh`, `app.sh` with no arguments, `npm start` and `npm run open` open the desktop app. `stop.sh` and `npm run stop` send a Quit request through Electron's single-instance mechanism. A pending renderer save can prevent closing; the API stays available while closing is prevented. Once the window closes, the server finishes requests, SQLite closes and the internal port is released. Default source startup uses the existing repository database; no timetable migration/reset is performed. Chromium profile/cache uses the dedicated per-user `Lecture Ledger` directory (normally `~/.config/Lecture Ledger` on Linux), separate from Git and other Electron apps; `.ledger/desktop.log` records background startup errors. Tests override `LEDGER_DB_PATH`, `LEDGER_DESKTOP_PROFILE` and `LEDGER_RUNTIME_DIR` to temporary locations, and smoke mode checks paths before opening the database.

Startup installs missing dependencies and rebuilds the frontend plus bundled Node backend when their fingerprint changes. Electron 44 downloads its binary on first use, separate from the npm package install. First installation needs internet; later starts work offline apart from external university links. There is no packaged distributable installer or automatic updater in this change; the installed shortcut launches this source checkout with its installed Electron runtime.

The shell controls share suitable Node selection through `scripts/node.sh`: Node on PATH or the bundled Node on this computer. Optional `LEDGER_NODE_BIN` selects an executable. The bundled development runtime was `/home/dz/.cache/codex-runtimes/codex-primary-runtime/dependencies/node/bin/node` (24.19.0); system Node was 20.20.0, so plain npm commands may require a suitable Node first. On other systems with Node 24.15+, use `npm ci` and `npm start`. Electron supports Linux/macOS/Windows; generated desktop shortcuts are Linux-specific. No sandbox-disabling flags are used.

Optional browser hosting: `npm run browser` is foreground (Ctrl+C stops it); `npm run browser:open` launches a background browser server; `npm run browser:stop` or `./app.sh stop` stops it. `./app.sh status` reports its address. The legacy `./app.sh menu` graphical menu controls this optional browser mode. Its preferred port defaults to 3000, can be saved with `./app.sh port 3001` in ignored `ledger.config.json`, and falls back through the next 20 ports when busy. `PORT` is an exact one-run browser override. These preferences and browser controls do not affect desktop mode. Browser state/authentication/launch-lock protections remain in `.ledger/`, and abandoned locks recover. Neither mode signals unrelated saved PIDs.

`LEDGER_DB_PATH` selects a database for either mode (prefer an absolute filename). The source app defaults to `<repository>/data/lecture-ledger.sqlite`; changing ports never changes it. Browser and desktop modes can share it with existing revision checks. `npm run dev` serves the browser frontend/API with live frontend updates and the browser port preference; stop it with Ctrl+C. Always use separate data for development/QA that modifies the planner. Browser tests also isolate `LEDGER_CONFIG_PATH` and `LEDGER_RUNTIME_DIR`.

Branding compatibility: Electron now displays Lecture Tracker and uses the new desktop entry identity, while intentionally retaining its existing `Lecture Ledger` profile path. Keep `data/lecture-ledger.sqlite`, `.ledger/`, `ledger.config.json`, `LEDGER_*` overrides, private IPC/bridge/control identifiers and the `lecture-ledger` backup payload format stable. New downloads use `lecture-tracker-backup.json`; both filename prefixes are ignored by Git. Close and reopen an already-running app to pick up renamed UI/menu strings.

The database survives closing the browser and restarting the server. SQLite uses WAL and may create sidecar files. The database file has owner-only permissions on the tested Linux system. Data, sidecars, dependencies, build output, environment files, local port settings, private runtime state/logs, and conventionally named backups are ignored by Git. Shutdown lets requests finish, with a five-second limit for lingering HTTP connections, before closing SQLite. A source clone does not include the user's timetable.

Export/restore in Term & settings or use:

```sh
npm run export -- /path/to/lecture-ledger-backup.json
npm run import -- /path/to/lecture-ledger-backup.json
```

Export refuses to overwrite a file; import refuses to replace a nonempty timetable without `--replace`. Back up before replacing data. Stop the app before copying the raw database and any remaining sidecars. Never reset or commit the real database for testing.

